{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":68.08999633789062,"text":"The Emperor Hadrian built this enormous stone drum on the bank of the Tiber between A.D. 134 and 139 to be the tomb of himself and his family. On top, it had a garden and a golden chariot with four horses, and in front of it, a bridge, which still stands. When Rome later needed protecting, the thick walls of the tomb became a castle. Legend says that in 590, when a sickness was troubling the city, the Pope saw an angel on its summit putting his sword back in its sheath, a sign that the sickness was ending, and the castle has carried the angel's name ever since. A bronze angel from 1753 still stands up there. The Popes made it their refuge. A covered corridor 800 meters long, the Pasetto, runs along an old wall all the way to the Vatican, and in 1527, Pope Clement VII ran through it when foreign soldiers broke into the city. The castle was a prison, too. In 1901, it became a museum, and today, you climb Hadrian's spiral ramp up to the terrace with the angel and all of Rome below. The ten angels on the bridge were designed by Bernini in 1668.","words":[{"word":"The","start":0,"end":0.4399999976158142},{"word":"Emperor","start":0.4399999976158142,"end":0.800000011920929},{"word":"Hadrian","start":0.800000011920929,"end":1.3799999952316284},{"word":"built","start":1.3799999952316284,"end":1.8600000143051147},{"word":"this","start":1.8600000143051147,"end":2.119999885559082},{"word":"enormous","start":2.119999885559082,"end":2.759999990463257},{"word":"stone","start":2.759999990463257,"end":3.2200000286102295},{"word":"drum","start":3.2200000286102295,"end":3.440000057220459},{"word":"on","start":3.440000057220459,"end":3.6600000858306885},{"word":"the","start":3.6600000858306885,"end":3.9800000190734863},{"word":"bank","start":3.9800000190734863,"end":4.019999980926514},{"word":"of","start":4.019999980926514,"end":4.159999847412109},{"word":"the","start":4.159999847412109,"end":4.579999923706